    "explore": {
      "text": "Play C, E and G over and over. Hum along with each key: first as quietly as a whisper, then as loudly as you dare. Last, start whisper-quiet and get louder with every note. The keys keep one volume; your voice does the changing.",
      "instrument": "keyboard",
      "exhibit": {
        "kind": "keyboard",
        "highlight": [
          "C4",
          "E4",
          "G4"
        ],
        "low": "C4",
        "high": "C5"
      },
      "goal": "Hum along as you play: quietly, then loudly, then getting louder."
    },
    "name": {
      "cards": [
        {
          "text": "Music has Italian words for volume. [[piano|Piano]] means quiet and is written *p*. [[forte|Forte]] means loud and is written *f*. Add [[mezzo|mezzo]], which means half or medium, and you get [[mezzo-piano|mezzo piano]] (*mp*, fairly quiet) and [[mezzo-forte|mezzo forte]] (*mf*, fairly loud)."
        },
        {
          "text": "Double the letter for an extreme. [[pianissimo|Pianissimo]] (*pp*) is very quiet. [[fortissimo|Fortissimo]] (*ff*) is very loud. Quietest to loudest: *pp*, *p*, *mp*, *mf*, *f*, *ff*. To remember: *p* is quiet, *f* is loud, and more letters means more of it."
        },
        {
          "text": "Volume can change gradually. [[crescendo|Crescendo]] means getting louder. [[decrescendo|Decrescendo]] means getting quieter, and so does [[diminuendo|diminuendo]]. They are written *cresc.*, *decresc.* and *dim.* A long thin wedge under the notes shows the same: opening out to the right is louder, closing in is quieter."
        }
      ],
      "alternative": {
        "text": "Picture a storm. Far away it is a faint rumble: *pp*. It comes closer, getting louder: a [[crescendo|crescendo]]. Overhead it roars: *ff*. Then it drifts off and fades: a [[diminuendo|diminuendo]], back down to *pp*. Each *f* adds loudness. Each *p* adds quiet."
      }
    },
